  #line-at(522.12pt, 81.34pt, ". ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 95.87pt, "Our findings show that the temperature increase in the LC sample can be much higher than that ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 110.41pt, "expected in aqueous solutions, enabling a better understanding of the thermal processes in STEM of ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 124.74pt, "nonaqueous  liquid samples  . This paper is divided into three sections. First, we present LP-STEM ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 139.28pt, "imaging of LC and describe the processes of phase transitions, defect formation, and surface effects. ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 153.81pt, "Then, we discuss the thermal origin of the observed phase changes supported by heat diffusion ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 168.35pt, "calculations. The thermal and radiolysis effects are discussed based on control experiments with ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 182.89pt, "additional laser heating, and via process reversibility. The paper concludes with a discussion of the ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 197.2pt, "contrast seen in the images and the perspectives this implies for LP-STEM imaging of LC materials. The ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 211.75pt, "results advance our fundamental understanding of LCs and offer insight into how LP-STEM can be used ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 226.29pt, "in further research on LC materials, emphasizing their relevance to nanocomposites and novel ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 240.83pt, "functional materials. Finally, manipulating the structure of a liquid crystal by an electron beam suggests ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 255.36pt, "the prospect of control at the nanoscale and design of advanced materials.  ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12.038pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
