#original-page(595.31pt, 841.97pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 10.902pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(292.2pt, 790.412pt, "11 ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 81.34pt, "properties, or the material is capable of absorbing more energy from the electron beam than it is ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 95.87pt, "estimated using stopping power, or both. There is also no doubt that the mechanism of pulsed energy ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 110.41pt, "delivery by STEM itself may be difficult to represent as a continuous heat source. This topic ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 124.74pt, "undoubtedly deserves further exploration. ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 10.902pt, weight: "bold", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.887pt, 147.23pt, "In situ laser-induced heating  ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 10.902pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.887pt, 169.71pt, "The relatively high temperature increase we require to explain the phase transitions in 8CB LC (with a ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 184.25pt, "maximum of about 15°C for the N-I transition) during these LP-STEM experiments might raise some ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 198.79pt, "concerns")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 7.041pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(110.88pt, 195.38pt, "64")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 10.902pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(118.15pt, 198.79pt, ". However, we have assumed in the calculations that any effects are due only to sample ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 213.34pt, "heating from the electron-beam irradiation, without considering radiolytic effects.  According to the ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 227.88pt, "literature, for bulk 8CB tested in a 7000 keV linear electron accelerator, high electron doses (in the ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 242.19pt, "order of 6 kGy and more) cause the material to undergo partial radiolysis, inducing detachment of CN ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 256.73pt, "groups from some 8CB molecules (8CB")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 7.041pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(240.85pt, 253.32pt, "+")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 10.902pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(244.25pt, 256.73pt, ") by chemical bond cracking")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 7.041pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(366.27pt, 253.32pt, "91")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 10.902pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(373.54pt, 256.73pt, ".  Such effects were found to affect ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 271.26pt, "the thermal properties of the mesophases, phase transition temperatures, electrooptic and dielectric ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 285.8pt, "anisotropies and molecular relaxation frequency depending on the used dose conditions")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 7.041pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(464.64pt, 282.39pt, "66")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 10.902pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(471.93pt, 285.8pt, ". ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 308.31pt, "It is therefore important to compare the phenomena we have observed under electron beam ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 322.84pt, "irradiation, which may also involve irreversible radiolysis effects, with transformations driven only by ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 337.15pt, "heating. To accomplish this, we therefore developed a new imaging approach . We performed control ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 351.69pt, "experiments with a laser, working at 445 nm wavelength, as an additional heating source during STEM ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 366.22pt, "imaging. The procedure is described in Methods and Fig. S6. It is important to emphasize that in our ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 380.78pt, "system, the entire sample was illuminated, not just the chosen (S)TEM imaging area. Generally, pure ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 395.32pt, "8CB does not absorb 445 nm wavelength light")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 7.041pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(292.43pt, 391.91pt, "91")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 10.902pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(299.7pt, 395.32pt, ". Therefore, it can be assumed that the overall ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 409.63pt, "temperature increase of the LC was induced by heating of the sample surrounding substrates, i.e., ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 424.17pt, "amorphous carbon layers and Cu grids. This approach provides more uniform heating across the entire ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 438.7pt, "system, without accounting for local effects arising from direct light absorption by the LC. We note that ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 453.26pt, "polarized laser light is known to induce the reorientation of LC molecules in the nematic phase via third ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 467.8pt, "order nonlinear optical effect called optical Freedericksz transition (OFT)")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 7.041pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(406.04pt, 464.39pt, "92,93")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 10.902pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(422.39pt, 467.8pt, ". Observing this effect ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 482.11pt, "requires much higher light powers than those used in this experiment. Additionally, the substantial light ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 496.64pt, "scattering and reflection inside the microscope column significantly reduce the initial laser beam's ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 511.18pt, "polarization.  ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 533.66pt, "We captured and analyzed LP-STEM images with the laser switched on and off. We found that the laser ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 548.22pt, "highly accelerated the phase transition sequence. This made it difficult to record and compare specific ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 562.76pt, "phase changes so we performed the experiments in a series of steps.  ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 585.25pt, "First, the electron beam induced the standard SmA-N and N-I phase transition sequence at the dose ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 599.55pt, "rate of 2.72 e")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 7.041pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(130.19pt, 596.15pt, "-")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 10.902pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(132.23pt, 599.55pt, "/nm")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 7.041pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(151.09pt, 596.15pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 10.902pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(154.72pt, 599.55pt, "s (Fig. 4a). Then, the laser light was switched on once the isotropic phase appeared ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 614.09pt, "(Fig. 4b). The experiment was performed this way, because the laser highly accelerated the phase ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 628.65pt, "transition sequence, making it impossible to record and compare specific phase changes. The laser ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 643.19pt, "illumination caused a vast increase in I phase volume in the observation area (of about 70 %, Fig. 5be), ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 657.72pt, "which occurred in a time shorter than one full STEM image frame in the series (i.e. 0.84 s, see ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 672.03pt, "supplementary video 5")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 7.041pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(174.28pt, 668.62pt, "82")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 10.902pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(181.54pt, 672.03pt, "). With further light illumination for 15 seconds, the I phase covered almost ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 686.57pt, "100% of the observation area. Switching off the laser light resulted in an instant decrease in ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 701.13pt, "temperature and a shrinkage of the I phase by nearly 60% (Fig. 4ce). The structure of the N and I phase ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 715.66pt, "mixture was inhomogeneous and composed of I phase and small droplets of N phase inside phase I, ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 730.2pt, "meaning that I-N transformation happened quite uniformly within the sample. This feature is a result ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 744.509pt, "of a sudden temperature drop, contrasting with N-I transformation observed in the previous ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 759.045pt, "experiments with slow heating. With further electron beam irradiation, the I phase then propagated ")
]

