  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 10.902pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(292.2pt, 790.412pt, "13 ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 81.34pt, "phase percentage in the observation area in time with laser switched on and off. For the full-time fast ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 95.87pt, "series, see Supplementary video 5")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 7.041pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(222.68pt, 92.47pt, "82")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 10.902pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(229.95pt, 95.87pt, ". ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 10.902pt, weight: "bold", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.887pt, 118.36pt, "Reversibility of phase transformations ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 10.902pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.887pt, 140.87pt, "As the I-N phase transformation was observed upon switching off the laser, an additional experiment ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 155.4pt, "was conducted to assess the reversibility of the process. Single STEM images were taken during the ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 169.71pt, "cooling the sample after switching off the electron beam. The same area was imaged with and without ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 184.25pt, "constant additional laser heating at a wavelength of 445 nm. The reversibility was measured by ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 198.79pt, "estimating the I phase content in the observation area [%] and its decrease in time. The images were ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 213.34pt, "taken with a relatively low dose of 2.28 e")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 7.041pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(255.38pt, 209.94pt, "-")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 10.902pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(257.43pt, 213.34pt, "/nm")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 7.041pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(276.28pt, 209.94pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 10.902pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(279.91pt, 213.34pt, " per image to minimize the effect of the single scan on ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 227.88pt, "the reversibility process. The raw images were edited for enhanced visibility of changes by subtracting ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 242.19pt, "a reference image of the 100% N phase. The resulting processed images are presented in the first rows ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 256.73pt, "of Fig. 5ab. Their false-colored versions are also displayed in the bottom rows for easier brightness ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 271.26pt, "change interpretation, where warmer colors indicate higher brightness in the grey images. The raw ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 285.8pt, "data is provided in Fig. S5 in the Supplementary Information. ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 308.31pt, "In case of no additional heating, the greatest decrease in I phase content (by about 90%, see Fig. 5ac) ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 322.84pt, "in the first 90 s of cooling (equivalent to beam-off time in Fig. 5). With further cooling the I phase shrinks ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 337.15pt, "circularly and almost disappears completely after 900 s (Fig. 5a).  Different results were obtained in ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 351.69pt, "case of simultaneous laser irradiation. The largest decrease (about 40 %) was still observed within the ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 366.22pt, "first 90 s after switching off the electron beam (Fig. 5bc). After that time, the laser illumination was ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 380.78pt, "long enough (time equivalent to beam-off time) to induce further growth of the I phase despite the ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 395.32pt, "electron beam's absence. An interesting contrast feature is observed with longer laser irradiation times, ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 409.63pt, "where the differences between the N and I phases become less distinguishable. With extended laser ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 424.17pt, "irradiation in the absence of an electron beam, the overall brightness of the images increases (Fig. 5b), ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 438.7pt, "leading to an almost invisible phase boundary between the N and I phases as the sample approaches ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 453.26pt, "the transition temperature. This is probably a result of constant, relatively slow heating of the whole ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 467.8pt, "sample. The brightening of the N phase can be related to the effects described in Fig. 2, where the ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 482.11pt, "intensity profile indicates a vast increase in brightness right before the start of the N-I transition.  In the ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 496.64pt, "case of localized electron irradiation only (Fig. 2b), the effect is mostly visible in the center, while in ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 511.18pt, "case of homogenous laser heating (Fig. 5b) the whole N phase surrounding I phase becomes brighter.  ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 525.72pt, "It is also possible that minor thickness changes take place due LC movements induced by a temperature ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 540.27pt, "rise.  ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 562.76pt, "We also note that before laser illumination an image of the region surrounding the area scanned in Fig. ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 577.07pt, "5a, taken at lower magnification (Fig. 5d), revealed some N phase near around the scanned area and ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 591.61pt, "unaffected SmA phase at further distances. Such observation suggests that the heat dissipates strongly ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 606.14pt, "from the imaging area, leading to I phase formation mainly in the center.  ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 628.65pt, "The results of the experiments with laser heating show that the N-I phase change is fully reversible ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 643.19pt, "once the heating sources are removed, leading to a few  conclusions. First, the observed processes are ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 657.72pt, "thermally induced, and the heat caused by the electron beam scanning the field of view dissipates in ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 672.03pt, "the X and Y axes out of the observation area. This leads to a higher temperature increase in the center, ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 686.57pt, "where the N-I phase change appears  first. In case of additional heating with a laser, the process of N-I ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 701.13pt, "is accelerated significantly. The presence of a two-phase mixture, with oval areas of the N phase ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 715.66pt, "suspended within the I phase, indicates that rapid cooling upon turning off the laser alters the ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 730.2pt, "appearance of the I-N transition. ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 752.685pt, "According to the literature")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 7.041pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(187.22pt, 749.279pt, "91")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 10.902pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(194.49pt, 752.685pt, ", an electron beam with a dose on the order of magnitude of 6 kGy or more ")
  #line-at(70.887pt, 766.995pt, "can induce the detachment of the CN group, leading to the formation of a cation 8CB")
